  "output": "Incident Analysis: Queue Depth Surge on 2026-09-08 (09:00–09:45)\n\n1. Primary Cause Identification\nThe evidence clearly indicates that dependency B rate limiting (HTTP 429 responses) was the primary cause of the queue incident. At 09:10, queue depth spiked from 120 to 1,850 while worker count remained at 8 (E01, E02). By 09:15, dependency B was returning HTTP 429 for 31% of requests (E05). The timeline shows the rate limiting began before the retry storm was observed at 09:16, establishing a clear causal sequence: dependency B began throttling, which triggered the retry mechanism, which amplified the problem.\n\n2. Role of the Retry Policy as Amplifier\nThe retry policy was the primary amplifier of the incident. At 09:16, retry volume increased by 420% (E06), meaning each original failed request spawned approximately 5.2 total attempts (1 original + 4.2 retries on average). This created a self-reinforcing loop: dependency B returned 429, the client retried, the retry counted against the rate limit, generating more 429s and more retries. The exponential backoff with a maximum of three retries was insufficient to dampen the surge because the backoff windows were too short relative to the rate limit recovery period.\n\n3. Why Adding Workers Had Limited Effect\nIncreasing workers from 8 to 16 reduced queue depth by only 6% over ten minutes (E07). This is consistent with a downstream bottleneck rather than a worker-capacity bottleneck. The workers were not CPU-bound (43% utilization), memory-bound (51%), or disk-bound (22%) (E04). They were blocked waiting on dependency B. Adding workers increased concurrent requests to dependency B, which likely worsened rate limiting rather than alleviating it. The system was not constrained by processing capacity but by downstream throughput.\n\n4. Retry Policy Assessment\nThe current retry policy is dangerously aggressive for rate-limited scenarios. The 420% retry volume increase (E06) indicates the policy lacks 429-aware backoff, retry budget limits, or circuit breaker logic. When retries were disabled for dependency B, queue depth fell 38% in six minutes (E08), confirming the retry policy was the dominant load amplifier. However, disabling retries increased the direct failure rate, indicating the system has no graceful degradation mode that preserves user experience while protecting downstream dependencies.\n\n5. Recovery Timeline and Root Cause Resolution\nQueue depth returned below 150 within eleven minutes after dependency B raised its rate limits at 09:35 and HTTP 429 responses fell below 1% (E09). This confirms the root cause was the rate limit threshold, not any internal system defect. No deploy, schema change, host restart, network route change, or credential rotation occurred during the incident window (E10), ruling out internal triggers.\n\n6. Alternative Hypothesis: Worker GC Pressure\nAn alternative hypothesis is that workers experienced garbage collection pauses or memory pressure causing task timeouts, which accumulated tasks in the queue. This is less supported by the evidence: memory utilization was only 51% (E04), and disabling retries produced immediate improvement (E08), which would not occur if the problem were worker-side. Additionally, the recovery correlated with dependency B rate limit changes, not worker restarts or configuration changes.\n\n7. Uncertainties\nSeveral uncertainties remain: (a) the exact rate limit threshold and window used by dependency B is unknown; (b) the distribution of 429 responses across dependency B endpoints is unclear; (c) whether the queue depth increase at 09:10 was caused by increased inbound traffic or decreased processing rate cannot be determined from the evidence; (d) the retry backoff configuration (base delay, jitter) is not specified; (e) whether dependency B communicated rate limit changes proactively or reactively is unknown.\n\n8. Remediation Recommendations\nImmediate actions: implement 429-aware exponential backoff with jitter and a per-dependency retry budget; add circuit breaker logic to pause requests when dependency B error rates exceed a threshold. Short-term: coordinate with dependency B to obtain documented rate limits and implement client-side throttling aligned to those limits. Medium-term: implement adaptive concurrency limits based on downstream latency and error rates rather than static worker counts.\n\n9. Risks\nWithout changes, a future rate limit reduction by dependency B will trigger the same cascading failure. The retry policy will amplify any downstream degradation into a queue backlog. The system lacks backpressure signaling, meaning queue growth is unbounded until memory exhaustion. Alerting thresholds were not triggered before the queue reached crisis levels.\n\n10. Edge Cases\nIf dependency B applies rate limits per-endpoint rather than globally, some task types may be disproportionately affected. If the retry policy uses fixed backoff without jitter, synchronized retry storms are likely. If dependency B rate limits are time-window-based, request bursts at window boundaries could trigger false throttling. If the system scales horizontally, each instance's retry policy multiplies the load on dependency B independently.", "data": {
